"A doctor of Chinese medicine traditionally begins an examination with observation, before a single question is asked, and the five-element framework gives that observation a specific vocabulary. Each element is matched not only to an organ, but to a facial color and a sense organ, treating the face as a kind of visible display panel for what's happening deeper inside the body.": "cad7674d13ee",
"A doctor of Chinese medicine traditionally begins an examination with observation, before a single question is asked, and the five-element framework gives that observation a specific vocabulary. Each element is matched not only to an organ, but to a facial color and a sense organ, treating the face as a kind of visible display panel for what's happening deeper inside the body. Wood is matched to a greenish or bluish tinge and to the eyes; a person under prolonged liver strain is traditionally said to show it around the eyes first. Fire is matched to redness and the tongue, tying the heart to speech and to a flushed complexion. Earth is matched to a yellowish tinge and the mouth, connecting digestive strength to the color around the lips. Metal is matched to paleness and the nose, and water is matched to a darker, sometimes bluish-black tinge and the ears. None of this works as a single glance diagnosis — a person's natural skin tone, lighting, and momentary flush from heat or exercise all complicate a reading taken in isolation. The framework's real use is the same as everywhere else in this course: not a single clue treated as conclusive, but one thread in a larger pattern, cross-checked against everything else a trained practitioner asks and observes.": "38d1b8f0e302",
